<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="ws-right-triangle-trigonometry">
    <title>Right Triangle Trigonometry</title>
    <definition xml:id="def-six-trigonometric-functions-right-triangle">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider a right triangle whose short leg has length <m>a</m>, whose long leg has length <m>b</m>, and whose hypotenuse has length <m>c</m>. Let <m>\theta</m> be the angle opposite the short leg. 
            </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-standard-right-triangle">
                    <caption>A right triangle where the short leg has length <m>a</m>, the long leg has length <m>b</m>, and the hypotenuse has length <m>c</m>. 
                        The vertex of the angle opposite to side <m>a</m> is labeled <m>A</m>, the vertex of the angle opposite to side <m>b</m> is labeled <m>B</m>, and the vertex of the right angle is labeled <m>C</m>. 
                        The angle <m>\angle CAB</m> is labeled <m>\theta</m>.</caption>
                    <image source="trigonometry/standard-right-triangle.png" width="50%" decorative="yes"/>
                </figure>
            <p>
                The six trigonometric functions of the angle <m>\theta</m> are defined as follows:
                <md>
                    <mrow> \sin(\theta)\amp=\frac{a}{c}=\frac{\text{opposite}}{\text{hypotenuse}}\amp\csc(\theta)\amp=\frac{c}{a}=\frac{\text{hypotenuse}}{\text{opposite}}</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow> \cos(\theta)\amp=\frac{b}{c}=\frac{\text{adjacent}}{\text{hypotenuse}}\amp\sec(\theta)\amp=\frac{c}{b}=\frac{\text{hypotenuse}}{\text{adjacent}}</mrow>
                    <mrow></mrow>
                    <mrow> \tan(\theta)\amp=\frac{a}{b}=\frac{\text{opposite}}{\text{adjacent}}\amp\cot(\theta)\amp=\frac{b}{a}=\frac{\text{adjacent}}{\text{opposite}}</mrow>
                </md>                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <note>
        <p>
            Three of the six trigonometric functions can be remembered using the mnemonic SOH-CAH-TOA, where
            <md>
                <mrow>\text{SOH}:\amp\text{ Sine-Opposite-Hypotenuse}</mrow>
                <mrow>\text{CAH}:\amp\text{ Cosine-Adjacent-Hypotenuse}</mrow>
                <mrow>\text{TOA}:\amp\text{ Tangent-Opposite-Adjacent}</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </note>
    <example xml:id="exa-evaluating-trigonometric-functions-right-triangle" workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Given the right triangle pictured below, find the value of the six trigonometric functions of the angle <m>\theta</m>.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-33-56-65-triangle">
                <caption>A right triangle whose short leg has length 33, long leg has length 56, and <m>\theta</m> measures the side opposite the short leg.</caption>
                <image source="trigonometry/33-56-65-triangle.png" width="30%" decorative="yes"/>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        </example>
        <example xml:id="exa-evaluating-isosceles-triangle" workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Given the right triangle pictured below, find the value of the six trigonometric functions of the angle <m>\theta</m>.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-45-45-90-triangle">
                    <caption>A right triangle whose legs each have length 3, and <m>\theta=45^\circ</m> measures the angle opposite side <m>b</m>.</caption>
                    <image source="trigonometry/isosceles-triangle.png" width="30%" decorative="yes"/>
                    </figure>
            </statement>
            </example>
            <example xml:id="exa-evaluating-30-60-90-triangle" workspace="4in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Given the right triangle pictured below, find the value of the six trigonometric functions for both the angle <m>\alpha</m> and the angle <m>\beta</m>.
                    </p>
                    <figure xml:id="fig-30-60-90-triangle">
                        <caption>A right triangle whose short leg has length <m>1</m>, long leg has length <m>\sqrt{3}</m>, and hypotenuse has length <m>2</m>. <m>\alpha=60^\circ</m> measures the angle opposite the long leg, and <m>\beta</m> measures the angle opposite the short leg.</caption>
                        <image source="trigonometry/30-60-90-triangle.png" width="30%" decorative="yes"/>
                    </figure>
                </statement>
            </example>
            <table xml:id="table-trigonometric-functions-common-angles">
    <title>Trigonometric Functions of Common Angles</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
        <row header="yes">
            <cell><m>\theta</m></cell>
            <cell><m>30^\circ=\frac{\pi}{6}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>45^\circ=\frac{\pi}{4}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>60^\circ=\frac{\pi}{3}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><m>\sin(\theta)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><m>\cos(\theta)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{1}{2}</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell><m>\tan(\theta)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\sqrt{3}</m></cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
</table>
            <theorem xml:id="thm-reciprocal-identities">
                <title>Reciprocal Identities</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>\theta</m> be any angle. The <term>reciprocal identities</term> are given by the following equations:
                        <md>
                            <mrow>\sin(\theta)\amp=\frac{1}{\csc(\theta)}\amp \csc(\theta)\amp=\frac{1}{\sin(\theta)}</mrow>
                            <mrow>\cos(\theta)\amp=\frac{1}{\sec(\theta)}\amp \sec(\theta)\amp=\frac{1}{\cos(\theta)}</mrow>
                            <mrow>\tan(\theta)\amp=\frac{1}{\cot(\theta)}\amp \cot(\theta)\amp=\frac{1}{\tan(\theta)}</mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </theorem>
            <theorem xml:id="thm-quotient-identities">
                <title>Quotient Identities</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>\theta</m> be any angle. The <term>quotient identities</term> are given by the following equations:
                        <md>
                            <mrow>\tan(\theta)\amp=\frac{\sin(\theta)}{\cos(\theta)}</mrow>
                            <mrow>\cot(\theta)\amp=\frac{\cos(\theta)}{\sin(\theta)}</mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </theorem>
            <example xml:id="exa-evaluting-trigonometric-functions-reciprocal-quotient" workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Given that <m>\sin(\theta)=\frac{3}{5}</m>, find the values of the other five trigonometric functions of <m>\theta</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </example>
            <theorem xml:id="thm-pythagorean-identities" workspace="4in">
                <title>Pythagorean Identities</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>\theta</m> be any angle. The <term>Pythagorean identities</term> are given by the following equations:
                        <md>
                            <mrow>\sin^2(\theta)+\cos^2(\theta)\amp=1</mrow>
                            <mrow>1+\tan^2(\theta)\amp=\sec^2(\theta)</mrow>
                            <mrow>1+\cot^2(\theta)\amp=\csc^2(\theta)</mrow>
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </theorem>
<example xml:id="exa-using-pythagorean-identities" workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Given that <m>\sec(\theta)=\frac{5}{3}</m>, find the values of the other five trigonometric functions of <m>\theta</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</example>
<definition xml:id="def-complement-angles">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Two positive angles are <term>complementary angles</term> if the sum of their measures is <m>90^\circ</m> or <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m> radians.
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>
<theorem xml:id="thm-cofunction-identities">
    <title>Cofunction Identities</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>\theta</m> be an acute angle. The <term>cofunction identities</term> are given by the following equations:
            <md>
                <mrow>\sin(\theta)\amp=\cos\left(90^\circ-\theta\right)\amp \cos(\theta)\amp=\sin\left(90^\circ-\theta\right)</mrow>
                <mrow>\tan(\theta)\amp=\cot\left(90^\circ-\theta\right)\amp \cot(\theta)\amp=\tan\left(90^\circ-\theta\right)</mrow>
                <mrow>\sec(\theta)\amp=\csc\left(90^\circ-\theta\right)\amp \csc(\theta)\amp=\sec\left(90^\circ-\theta\right)</mrow>
            </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</theorem>
<example xml:id="exa-using-cofunction-identities" workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Find a cofunction with the same values as <m>\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</example>
<example xml:id="exa-using-calculator-degrees" workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Use a calculator to find the value of <m>\sin(37^\circ)</m>, rounded to four decimal places.
        </p>
    </statement>
</example>
<example xml:id="exa-using-calculator-radians" workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Use a calculator to find the value of <m>\sec\left(\frac{5\pi}{12}\right)</m>, rounded to four decimal places.
        </p>
    </statement>
</example>
<example xml:id="exa-using-calculator-inverse-trig" workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Use a calculator to find the approximate value (in degrees) of <m>\cos^{-1}(0.155)</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</example>
<definition xml:id="def-angle-elevation-depression">
    <statement>
    <p>
        <ul>
            <li>The <term>horizontal line of sight</term> is the line formed when an observer looks straight ahead.</li>
            <li>The <term>angle of elevation</term> is the angle formed between the horizontal line of sight and the line of sight to an object above the observer.</li>
            <li>The <term>angle of depression</term> is the angle formed between the horizontal line of sight and the line of sight to an object below the observer.</li>
        </ul>
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-angle-elevation-depression">
        <caption>An observer is at a vertex shared by two right triangles. 
            The horizontal line of sight from the observer forms a common side shared by both triangles. 
            The line of sight to an object above the observer forms the hypotenuse of the upper triangle, and the line of sight to an object below the observer forms the hypotenuse of the lower triangle.
            The angle formed by using the horizontal line of sight as the initial side and the line of sight to the above object as the terminal side is the angle of elevation.
            The angle formed by using the horizontal line of sight as the initial side and the line of sight to the below object as the terminal side is the angle of depression.
        </caption>
        <image source="trigonometry/angle-elevation-depression.png" width="50%" decorative="yes"/>
    </figure>
    </statement>
    </definition>
    <example xml:id="exa-angle-elevation" workspace="4in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            An observer stands 150 feet away from the base of a building. The angle of elevation from the observer to the top of the building is <m>35^\circ</m>. Find the height of the building, rounded to the nearest foot.
        </p>
        </statement>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="exa-finding-angle-depression" workspace="4in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            An observer stands on the edge of a cliff that is 200 feet above sea level and observes a boat that is 150 feet away from the base of the cliff. Find the angle of depression from the observer to the boat, rounded to the nearest tenth of a degree.
        </p>
        </statement>
    </example>
</worksheet>